{"id":90654382831690,"type":"genre","name":"Minneapolis Sound","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":71,"info":{"img":null},"own_scenes":[{"id":271,"name":"Prince y el sonido Mineápolis","book":{"id":4,"slug":"Funk_Vincent","title":"Funk: The Music, the People, and the Rhythm of the One","author":"Rickey Vincent","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/Funk/","families":{"raices":{"name":"Raíces: blues, jazz, gospel y R&B","color":"#d97706"},"soul":{"name":"Soul y proto-funk: Stax, Nueva Orleans, Motown","color":"#facc15"},"jb":{"name":"James Brown y los JB’s","color":"#ef4444"},"rock":{"name":"Funk psicodélico y black rock: Sly, Hendrix","color":"#c084fc"},"fusion":{"name":"Jazz-funk y fusión","color":"#38bdf8"},"bandas":{"name":"Bandas de funk de los setenta","color":"#22c55e"},"pfunk":{"name":"P-Funk: Parliament-Funkadelic","color":"#a3e635"},"disco":{"name":"Disco y dance funk","color":"#f472b6"},"ochenta":{"name":"Funk de los ochenta: Prince, Rick James, electro","color":"#22d3ee"},"hiphop":{"name":"Hip hop, go-go y funk de los noventa","color":"#94a3b8"},"cultura":{"name":"Política y cultura negra","color":"#e5e7eb"}}},"family":"Funk de los ochenta: Prince, Rick James, electro","color":"#22d3ee","start":1978,"end":1995,"place":"Mineápolis (Paisley Park)","desc":"Prince Rogers Nelson, hijo de músicos de Mineápolis, obtuvo de Warner el control creativo total a los diecisiete años y cambió la música del país como nadie desde James Brown. Arreglos desnudos como los de Rick James, voz de gospel, todos los instrumentos y un erotismo descarado («ningún negro jugaba así con la sensibilidad heterosexual desde Little Richard»): «Soft and Wet», «I Wanna Be Your Lover», «Head» de Dirty Mind (1980), «Erotic City». Warner lo vendió con Zapp como «New Black Funk». Controversy, 1999 («Let’s Work», «D.M.S.R.») y Purple Rain (1984), película y plataforma de satélites (Vanity, Apollonia, Sheila E., The Family). The Time de Morris Day («Cool», «777-9311», «Jungle Love») fijaron el listón de precisión del naked funk, y de ellos salieron Jesse Johnson y Jimmy Jam y Terry Lewis, productores de Janet Jackson, New Edition o S.O.S. Band. Luego vinieron mundos fantásticos al nivel de P-Funk o los Beatles (Around the World in a Day, Parade, Sign o’ the Times, Lovesexy). El psicólogo Alvin Poussaint lo llamó «flautista de Hamelín de una generación obsesionada por el sexo», quizá preparada así para el gangsta rap.","aka":["Minneapolis sound","The Time","Morris Day","Jimmy Jam & Terry Lewis","Purple Rain"],"notes":[],"influences":[{"name":"Rick James","id":247816445291452,"type":"artist"},{"name":"Sly and the Family Stone","id":80661023040313,"type":"artist"},{"name":"Jimi Hendrix","id":144732909556490,"type":"artist"},{"name":"James Brown, el Padrino del Soul","id":75047647422289,"type":"genre"}],"refs":[{"ch":20,"q":"Their judgment proved right as Prince changed the country’s music as much as any one man since James Brown.","para":"Born Prince Rogers Nelson in Minneapolis, Minnesota, to musical parents (both black), Prince had his own band in high school and was a perfectionist even in his first dealings with Warner Brothers in 1978, who amazingly obliged him with complete creative control as a seventeen-year-old unproven act. Their judgment proved right as Prince changed the country’s music as much as any one man since James Brown.","page":339,"pdf":275,"chapter":"Cap. 19: Funk in the 1980s: Super Freaks"},{"ch":20,"q":"The Minneapolis sound became a musical movement.","para":"The Minneapolis sound became a musical movement. The high-pitched voices, the synth-bass and nasty splinking guitar licks, the slick pop percussion, and the brazen sexual themes created a narcissistic subculture all its own for the terminally horny. Subsequent Prince records began to develop vast fantasyscapes with the ambitious scope of P-Funk or the Beatles. These records—Around the World in a Day in 1985, Parade in 1986, Sign of the Times in 1987, Lovesexy and the Black Album in 1988, Graffiti Bridge in 1990, and Diamonds and Pearls in 1991—were all strong examples of the genius of Prince, capable of taking the listener to another world.","page":342,"pdf":277,"chapter":"Cap. 19: Funk in the 1980s: Super Freaks"},{"ch":20,"q":"What separated Prince from other artists of the 1980s was his ability to create grand visions, entire worlds of erotic indulgence and freedom.","para":"What separated Prince from other artists of the 1980s was his ability to create grand visions, entire worlds of erotic indulgence and freedom. He explored with authority the range of bizarre and carnal emotions that most of us keep hidden. He told an interviewer in a 1981 Jet magazine story that his work is motivated by the “despair” and “loneliness” he sees around him, and that he is dedicated to addressing those feelings. Like in Clinton’s “Atomic Dog,” Prince’s sexually charged imagery penetrated the depressing energy of the times with an exhilarating, new erotic hype.","page":342,"pdf":277,"chapter":"Cap. 19: Funk in the 1980s: Super Freaks"}],"artists":[{"id":94857305338951,"type":"artist","name":"The Prince","rating":null},{"id":122674245749374,"type":"artist","name":"Mavis Staples","rating":null},{"id":19270955192767,"type":"artist","name":"The Family","rating":null},{"id":261949063480329,"type":"artist","name":"The Time","rating":null},{"id":147203779023005,"type":"artist","name":"Prince and The Revolution","rating":null},{"id":125963087555829,"type":"artist","name":"Sheila E.","rating":null},{"id":176948918323460,"type":"artist","name":"Prince and The New Power Generation","rating":null},{"id":28933403301281,"type":"artist","name":"Apollonia 6","rating":null},{"id":73325978204214,"type":"artist","name":"Jesse Johnson","rating":null},{"id":111354271527545,"type":"artist","name":"Morris Day","rating":null},{"id":23483303780190,"type":"artist","name":"André Cymone","rating":null},{"id":277327853729166,"type":"artist","name":"Vanity","rating":null},{"id":17621766770